To become the most knowledgeable person in human history about unconventional warfare, I would approach it as mastering the full spectrum of irregular conflict: insurgency, counterinsurgency, resistance movements, guerrilla warfare, psychological operations, intelligence, political warfare, unconventional statecraft, and the interaction between military and civilian systems.

Unconventional warfare is much broader than guerrilla fighting. It involves understanding how political movements, populations, governments, institutions, information networks, and armed organizations interact during conflict.

The objective is to understand the entire discipline academically, historically, strategically, and operationally.

